# Job Control Tests
#
# These exercise the &, jobs, bg, fg, and kill features described in
# ish.man.pdf. Job numbers and process IDs are assigned by the OS and can't
# be predicted, and the man page doesn't pin down the exact wording or
# spacing "jobs" or a completion report should use (only that a background
# job's start prints "[N] pid..."), so most checks here use shelltest's
# regexp mode (a bare `/pattern/` after `>`, which searches for the pattern
# anywhere in the actual output) instead of an exact match.
#
# Tests that need a real SIGTSTP (the signal CTRL-Z generates on a real
# terminal) live in job-control-signals.sh instead: shelltest feeds ish its
# input from a plain file, not a pseudo-terminal, so a literal ^Z byte in a
# < block here would never reach a terminal driver and never become a
# signal.

# Test that starting a job in the background reports its job number and
# process ID, e.g. "[1] 1234"
<
/bin/sleep 1 &
$ SHELL
> /\[1\] [0-9]+/

# Test that job numbers increase as more jobs are backgrounded
<
/bin/sleep 1 &
/bin/sleep 1 &
$ SHELL
> /\[2\] [0-9]+/

# Test that & returns to the prompt right away instead of waiting for the
# job to finish. If & blocked, this test would run past its CI timeout.
<
/bin/sleep 3 &
/bin/echo after
$ SHELL
> /after/

# Test that jobs lists a running background job by job number and command
<
/bin/sleep 2 &
jobs
$ SHELL
> /\[1\].*sleep/

# Test that a finished background job is reported before the next prompt.
# The foreground sleep gives the background job time to actually finish;
# unlike the bare "[1] pid" startup message, a completion report naming the
# command is something the shell has to notice and print on its own.
<
/bin/sleep 1 &
/bin/sleep 2
$ SHELL
> /\[1\].*sleep/

# Test that referencing a job number that doesn't exist is reported as an
# error rather than silently ignored or crashing. %99 is a job number, not a
# process ID: no job has been started here, so job 99 cannot exist.
<
fg %99
$ SHELL
PROMPT PROMPT exit
>2 /99/
